A question about intent that the code cannot answer.

The dashboard has nine editing actions for work items that live in GitHub: change the
title, the description, the stage, the priority, the assignee or the parent; add a
comment; close an item; create a new one. All nine are turned on or off together by a
single yes-or-no marker stored on the linked board.

Nothing anywhere in the product ever sets that marker. So it is always no, all nine
actions are unavailable, and every GitHub-backed board is displayed to the user with a
read-only badge on it.

There are only two explanations, and they call for opposite work.

If two-way editing of GitHub-backed work was never meant to ship, then what users see is
correct and honestly labelled, and the nine actions and the marker are dead weight that
should be deleted. That would also remove a body of write-capable code that nothing
currently exercises.

If two-way editing was meant to work, then it has never worked for anyone, and the way
it fails is the problem: the product does not error and does not warn, it puts a
confident read-only badge on the board.

One thing that should not be read either way. Nobody has ever complained about this.
That is not a sign the feature is unwanted, and it is not a sign it is fine. The badge
tells a user the limitation is deliberate, so a user who wanted to edit would conclude
they were never meant to and would say nothing. Both explanations predict exactly the
silence we have, so the silence tells us nothing about which one is right.

What I cannot get from the code: which of those was intended. There is no comment, no
test and no note recording a decision either way, so it is not recoverable by reading
anything we have.

The judgment I need: was editing GitHub-backed work from the dashboard ever intended to
be available?
